<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Magersfontein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Magersfontein was fought on 11 December 1899 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-cronje">Piet Cronjé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koos-de-la-rey">Koos de la Rey</a>, dug into trenches at the foot of the hills, repulsed a British attack under Lord Methuen. The defeat, which fell in the 'Black Week' of British reverses, halted the first attempt to relieve the siege of Kimberley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-magersfontein.jpg" alt="Battle of Magersfontein" width="278" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_in_Barbed_Wire_Magersfontein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Early in the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>, and Lord Methuen's 1st Division advanced along the railway from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> to relieve it, driving the Boers from their positions at Belmont, Graspan and the Modder River at a cost of a thousand casualties. While Methuen waited for the Modder railway bridge to be repaired, the Boers brought their force up to some 8,500 men. De la Rey, who had just buried his son Adriaan, persuaded President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martinus-theunis-steyn">Martinus Theunis Steyn</a> of the Orange Free State to move the defence forward to Magersfontein, and to dig the trenches in front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kopje">kopjes</a> rather than on their slopes, where British artillery expected to find them.</p><p>Unable to reconnoitre the position and without serviceable maps, Methuen believed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> held the crests. He planned a bombardment on the evening of 10 December, followed by a night march of the Highland Brigade under Major General Wauchope for a frontal attack at dawn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The bombardment fell on the empty slopes and only warned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>. In torrential rain, which also upset the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compasses</a>, the Highlanders marched in quarter column, 3,500 men packed into a block 45 yards wide. At first light they were still in that formation, and they were about 400 yards from the trenches when the Boers opened fire. Wauchope was killed by almost the first volley, and according to Conan Doyle 700 of the British casualties fell in the first five minutes.</p><p>The survivors lay pinned down under the summer sun for most of the day while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> tried to encircle them. The Seaforth Highlanders overran the Scandinavian Corps, a company of foreign volunteers whose stand Cronje later credited, next to God, with the victory. In the late afternoon the remaining Highlanders broke and fled, and Methuen withdrew his army to the Modder River camp during the night.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The British lost 22 officers and 188 men killed and 46 officers and 629 other ranks wounded, 747 of their losses falling on the Highland Brigade; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> losses are disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts">Lord Roberts</a> was made Commander in Chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> on 15 February 1900 and forced Cronje to surrender at Paardeberg on 27 February. Typhoid, spreading from the camps at Modder River and Paardeberg, killed more British soldiers in the war than enemy action.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Magersfontein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second Boer War the Free State mobilised its commandos alongside the Transvaal from October 1899, besieging Kimberley and Ladysmith and fighting at <b>Magersfontein</b> and Paardeberg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
